2026-06-30 11:05:02,279 [INFO] ("The variable 'Settlement Hierarchy' categorizes settlements into five levels: 1. Capital city (the largest urban center, often a political, legal, and trade hub, exemplified by London in 1485), 2. Major Cities (significant urban centers, exemplified by cities of around 10,000 people like Norwich in 1485), 3. Market towns (smaller urban centers, exemplified by towns of a few hundred to one thousand inhabitants serving local rural areas like Abingdon in 1485), 4. Villages (rural settlements, exemplified by 50-300 people), and 5. Hamlets (the smallest rural settlements). Considering this framework, what was the highest level of settlement hierarchy attained within '<polity>' during the period from <time-start> to <time-end>? Your response should be a single integer corresponding to the hierarchy level (1-5).", None, [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You are are history professor writing challenging questions for PhD students.\n\n    Your task is to create a challenging yet well-defined historical exam question based on a variable definition from a historical dataset.\n    Follow the rules below, and return ONLY the final question template, based on the examples provided. \n    \n    1. Begin the question with a concise explanation of the variable's definition, strictly adhering to the provided description, if no description is provided, use the variable name as the basis for the question.\n    2. Ensure all of the following template markers are used:\n        - '<polity>' for polity names (e.g. 'The Papal States')\n        - '<time-start>' and '<time-end>' for temporal scope\n    3. The question should make abundantly clear what the format of the desired response is and that only that response should be given.\n    4. The output must be exactly one question, ensuring that the question is fully complete in its length, and must end with a question mark (?).\n\n    Structure your response with a clear question template.\n    "}, {'role': 'us