ent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Settlement Hierarchy\n"Variable description":  levels.: Capital city: Winchester in the former kingdom of Wessex was the centre of royal and administrative power as well as housing the archbishop and cathedrals, three minsters and an episcopal palace.Coins were minted here. :: Major Towns:: Important larger market towns such as London, York, Cambridge, and Ely. They had cathedrals and were the official seat of a diocesan bishop.  ::: Port and trading towns::: Trading emporium and market towns and ports such as Dover, Sarre, Southampton, and Ipswich.  ::: Villages::: Villages were small and mostly consisted of farmland and a few hall-type buildings. They were generally situated with access to good ploughing land, but some were in lower-status areas such as on the margins of occupied districts or in dry or chalky areas. These may have been used by the Germanic immigrants to establish themselves.  :::: Monastic communities:::: Slightly more isolated and small communities based on a monastery with a self-sustaining ‘home-farm’ at its centre.  Double monasteries existed which housed monks and nuns side-by-side in communities ran by an abbess. After a slump in monastic life during the Viking invasions and colonisations of the Anglo-Saxon period, Benedictine monasteries surged in the tenth century. Sixty monasteries were established between 940 – 1066.  '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 2780, 'temperature': 0.2})
