. They had cathedrals and were the official seat of a diocesan bishop. ::: 3. Port and trading towns::: Trading emporium and market towns and ports such as Dover, Sarre, Southampton, and Ipswich.  ::: 4. Villages::: Villages were mostly situated with access to good ploughing land. Many smaller, more isolated villages were in lower-status areas such as on the margin of occupied districts or in dry or chalky areas. These may have been used by the Germanic immigrants to establish themselves.  :::: 5. Hamlets:::: Hamlets were the smallest of settlements, which generally consisted of a few hall-type buildings.  Village or hamlet settlements were established close together, usually within 2-5 kilometres of each other. ::::: 6. Monastic communities::::: Slightly more isolated and small communities based on a monastery with a self-sustaining ‘home-farm’ at its centre. Double monasteries existed which housed monks and nuns side-by-side in communities ran by an abbess.   '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 2521, 'temperature': 0.2})
